    "chunk_size_bytes": 1565,
    "chunk_text": "For someone who wrote so extensively on such a wide range of topics, it is astonishing how Wickramasinghe has been restricted today to a handful of novels and other works. Most Sri Lankans, especially Sinhala-speakers, identify him as the author of Madol Duwa, the Koggala Trilogy, and Viragaya. Even if they have not read these texts, it is as their author that Wickramasinghe is celebrated and venerated today, by scholars, students, and every other Sri Lankan. Part of the rationale for these lectures has been to compel audiences to think differently about him. They were timed for his 135th birth anniversary, which fell last year, and his 50th death anniversary, which falls this July.\n\nAs I went about brainstorming the modalities, including the timelines, I realised the research was going to be harder and that it was going to take longer than I thought. With patronage from the Martin Wickramasinghe Trust, I began work officially in November 2024 and went on until February the following year. Wrapping up preliminary research, including catalogues of Wickramasinghe\u2019s books and his newspaper articles, I commenced the second phase of the research with interviews. The first of these interviews was with Dr Sarath Amunugama; it was followed by conversations with 15 or so others who knew him, worked with him, or studied him. One of the most interesting of these interviews was with the ornithologist Professor Sarath Kotagama, who highlighted another aspect to Wickramasinghe which has been overlooked, his advocacy of simple science terms in Sinhala."
