    "language": "en",
    "md5": "52d4c9ed5acc4daa5558b84d2a6b384c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "By then I had read two books which helped me reframe the structure of this work: Shanti Jayewardene\u2019s excellent study of Geoffrey Bawa, Geoffrey Manning Bawa: Decolonializing Architecture, and SinhaRaja Tammita-Delgoda\u2019s equally exceptional study of George Keyt, Absence of a Desired Image. They had been published seven years of each other, and I had been involved in the latter as research coordinator. Both books, and their authors, got me to think anew about the format and aims of my study.\n\nIndeed, for the serious student of Sri Lankan and South Asian literature, he comes across as something of an enigma: a self-taught man, emerging from the backdrop of a deeply colonised society, discovering new modes of thinking and conveying them, in his language and in the language of the coloniser, English, to locals and foreigners.\n\nMy point is that Wickramasinghe\u2019s interests straddled different fields, and it would be wrong to limit his contribution to the 13 or so novels that bear his name. Yet it is also wrong to isolate them from the corpus of his other writings. As I mentioned in my Marga lecture, the Koggala Trilogy, which is touted as the definitive Sinhalese work of fiction, was rooted in his experiences in Koggala and Colombo: the transformations which had become a part of the city and the village in early 20th century Ceylon. Indeed, there are passages in Upan Da Sita, his autobiography, which give us interesting glimpses into the social and class structures of his village. Like Fernand Braudel exploring the history of France towards the end of his life, Wickramasinghe uses these anecdotes to make sense of a way of life, a way of looking at the world, a way of seeing, which helps us understand our past better."
  },
