    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s biodiversity: A living laboratory from backyard to ocean depths\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s extraordinary biodiversity \u2014 spanning everything from ants in home gardens to blue whales in its surrounding oceans \u2014 took centre stage at Thursday\u2019s public lecture that highlighted the island\u2019s status as one of the world\u2019s most remarkable natural treasure troves.\n\nThe lecture, titled \u201cThe Amazing Biodiversity of Sri Lanka: An Overview of the Plants and Animals on this Magical Island,\u201d was organised by the Wildlife and Nature Protection Society, in collaboration with Nations Trust Bank, and delivered by renowned naturalist Gehan de Silva Wijeyeratne, on Thursday, at the Bandaranaike Memorial International Conference Hall (BMICH).\n\nIn a wide-ranging and engaging presentation, Wijeyeratne took the audience on a journey across the tree of life, demonstrating how Sri Lanka functions as a living laboratory of evolution, with high levels of endemism and an astonishing diversity of species \u2014 many still inadequately studied.\n\n\u201cBiodiversity is not just about the big, charismatic animals people pay money to come and see. It is much wider \u2014 and often right there in your backyard,\u201d he stressed, setting the tone for a lecture that challenged conventional perceptions of wildlife.\n\nBeginning with insects, Wijeyeratne revealed a hidden world of complex interactions and extraordinary adaptations. Far from being insignificant, insects represent some of the most sophisticated ecological relationships found in nature.\n\nCertain butterfly species, for instance, engage in mutually beneficial relationships with ants. The caterpillars produce sugary secretions that attract ants, which in turn provide protection from predators. However, this relationship comes at a cost.\n\n\u201cYou give us honey, and in return the ants get to eat some of your babies. It\u2019s a bizarre trade-off, but that\u2019s how the animal world operates,\u201d he explained."
  },
