    "chunk_size_bytes": 1919,
    "chunk_text": "Wijeyeratne described recent decades as a \u201crenaissance\u201d in biodiversity research, with scientists uncovering a wealth of previously unknown species.\n\n\u201cThis work has transformed our understanding of Sri Lanka\u2019s biodiversity,\u201d he said.\n\nThe lecture also challenged common assumptions about reptiles and birds.\n\nSri Lanka is home to 34 endemic bird species, many of which display complex behaviours, such as cooperative breeding, where older offspring assist in raising younger siblings.\n\n\u201cThese behaviours are not just fascinating \u2014 they tell us a lot about evolution and survival strategies,\u201d he said.\n\nTurning to marine ecosystems, Wijeyeratne highlighted Sri Lanka\u2019s global significance for whale watching, particularly for blue whales \u2014 the largest animals ever to have lived.\n\n\u201cThey evolved from land mammals \u2014 something like a small deer that went back into the sea,\u201d he explained.\n\nSperm whales, he noted, represent extreme adaptations, capable of diving to great depths in total darkness.\n\n\u201cThey rely on echolocation, much like bats, because vision doesn\u2019t work in those conditions,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cPeople confuse poor visibility with poor biodiversity. We don\u2019t lack marine life \u2014 we are very rich,\u201d he emphasised.\n\nWijeyeratne acknowledged the growing importance of wildlife tourism, noting that Sri Lanka has seen a rapid increase in safari operators.\n\n\u201cWe\u2019ve gone from not recognising we are a top wildlife destination to perhaps realising it too well. Now we must manage it,\u201d he cautioned.\n\nWhile tourism presents economic opportunities, it also poses challenges that require careful regulation and sustainable practices.\n\nOn conservation, Wijeyeratne was candid about the challenges faced by communities living near protected areas.\n\n\u201cAt the end of the day, it comes down to money,\u201d he said, noting that economic pressures often drive environmental degradation."
