    "chunk_text": "On conservation, Wijeyeratne was candid about the challenges faced by communities living near protected areas.\n\n\u201cAt the end of the day, it comes down to money,\u201d he said, noting that economic pressures often drive environmental degradation.\n\nWijeyeratne also warned of the growing impact of climate change, which is already altering species distribution patterns.\n\nBird species, once confined to dry zones, are increasingly appearing in wetter regions, while those in mountainous areas face the risk of extinction.\n\n\u201cThings in the mountains can\u2019t go any higher \u2014 so they will disappear,\u201d he cautioned.\n\nConcluding his lecture, Wijeyeratne reminded Sri Lankans of the privilege of living in such a biologically rich country.\n\n\u201cWe are incredibly privileged to have this biodiversity, sometimes right in our own backyards,\u201d he said.\n\nHis message was clear: Sri Lanka\u2019s biodiversity is not only a scientific marvel but also a vital national asset \u2014 one that, if properly understood and managed, could play a crucial role in the country\u2019s environmental sustainability and economic future.\n\nThe lecture served as both a celebration of Sri Lanka\u2019s natural heritage and a call to action \u2014 urging greater awareness, stronger conservation efforts and a deeper appreciation of the island\u2019s unparalleled place in the natural world.\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
