    "md5": "4606f4e222faf706519bdb99de6fd737",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1717,
    "chunk_text": "If the taxpayer cannot pay, the tax is deemed a \"court fine.\" Failure to pay this fine allows the Magistrate to sentence the taxpayer to imprisonment. This creates a \u201cdebtors' prison\" for tax liabilities. Because Section 122(8A) may have already locked out the taxpayer's evidence at the assessment stage, they now enter the recovery stage legally barred from arguing that the debt is actually wrong. The Magistrate is reduced to a recovery agent for the IRD, tasked with jailing rather than adjudication.\n\nThe 2026 Bill adds a final layer of intimidation through the new Chapter XVIIA, which introduces summary criminal trials for five specific administrative acts: failing to file annual statements, failing to file income returns, failing to register, failing to appear for summons, and failing to furnish returns.\n\nHistorically, these failures were handled under Chapter XVII, which provides for administrative penalties, such as Rs. 50,000 for failing to register or up to Rs. 400,000 for late filing. While the Bill provides a mandatory 30-day warning notice before a trial begins, the \"oppression\" lies in the criminalisation of what were once civil defaults. If the notice is ignored \"without reasonable cause,\" the taxpayer is hauled before a Magistrate and faces imprisonment for up to six months, a criminal fine of Rs. 400,000, or both.\n\nThe illusion of protection: Section 186 and the coercive binary choice\n\nTaxpayers might look to Section 186(5) for relief. This section stipulates that no administrative penalty is payable if a person has been convicted of a criminal offence for the same conduct. Similarly, if an offence is compounded with the CGIR's consent, the administrative penalty is waived."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-03-23-dailyftlk-ac6f44dd",
    "num": "dailyftlk-ac6f44dd",
    "date_str": "2026-03-23",
    "description": "Procedural overreach in Inland Revenue 2026 Bill",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Procedural-overreach-in-Inland-Revenue-2026-Bill/4-789903",
