    "md5": "f1e5a5ac857fe8b114ee33af2c7ea0d0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1991,
    "chunk_text": "Trembling of tranquility in turbulent times: Woes of a weary war\n\nIn both good and bad times, leaders, managers, and administrators alike need to acknowledge the fact that they need to be still in order to be sharp and focused. It should not be an accident but a concentrated effort in committing time to be with oneself. Inward bound journey begins there\n\nThe ravaging war damaging the fragile chances of reaching an agreement among all the parties involved, has moved into the third week. As much as the colossal material damage in the Middle East, there is a rise of mental agony among affected millions. It is a time where truth is twisted by many fronts with vested interest and trust is shattered, also by many fronts with high unpredictability of next phase. Whilst, much is being written about the facets of on-going war, I thought of writing on peace, which is of very high demand, and particularly on \u201cinner peace.\u201d Let me reflect on needed tranquility in turbulent times that we go through.\n\nWhen the outer world is increasingly turbulent, how can we maintain inner tranquility? This might be the answer currently being sought by millions of affected masses worldwide, with multiple effects of war. The implications of it might include individuals and institutions alike with invitation to a better and a more serene outlook on life. Whilst experiencing multiple challenges due to global uncertainties, moving ahead with sound decisions and solid actions is much required.\n\nTranquility is simply the state of being calm. It involves stillness. Stillness is your essential nature, says Eckhart Toll, the Canadian author of spirituality fame. As he goes further:\n\nYou are that awareness, disguised as a person. When you lose touch with inner stillness, you lose touch with yourself. When you lose touch with yourself, you lose yourself in the world. Your innermost sense of self, of who you are, is inseparable from stillness. This is the I Am that is deeper than name and form."
  },
