    "chunk_size_bytes": 1848,
    "chunk_text": "A paid manager, by the name of Mr. Mariathasan was appointed. A non-existent shop out of Jaffna issued receipts on books certifying several millions in groceries where groceries for the destitute were \u201cbought.\u201d Cheques were signed by the treasurer Mr. Hariharan and the Rev. Jameson Gnanaponrajah (then Principal of St. John\u2019s as second signatory.)\n\nThis is how parish accounts are passed \u2013 at a meeting where the accounts are late and at a late second meeting a few of us barely meeting quorum approve the accounts. The Bishop and the Standing Committee give their approval. When challenged, the Bishop\u2019s lawyer tells the judge that they are always late and the polite judge is reluctant to tell the Bishop off. Or when retired Bishops are gifted free cars violating trust funds, the ignorant diocesan auditor has not even seen the constitutional restriction on trust funds. A parish auditor promised to sign the accounts before he had even seen them.\n\nThese blank cheque leaves signed by Gnanaponrajah were deposited into an account opened in a bank on the offshore islands. The bank manager would say that three men in cassocks came making him lower his usually instinctive guards in opening the account. It turns out he has no clue as to who they are. Multiple millions of tsunami funds were cleared this way.\n\nIt turns out that Gnanaponrajah, the second signatory on cheques excused himself saying he was so busy as principal that he signed blank cheques and gave them to the treasurer who misused them! Here we see the corrupt church working.\n\nGnanaponrajah was part of the fraud in signing those cheques for grocery purchases, or else he was fiduciarily in cahoots for signing cheques whose genuineness he had not verified. Either way he deserved sacking as a minimum punishment. But no action was taken. After all, he is a priest!"
