    "chunk_size_bytes": 1834,
    "chunk_text": "Navigating Sri Lanka\u2019s new VAT refund framework: Key compliance insights\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s transition to a risk-based Value Added Tax (VAT) refund system marks a significant shift in tax administration. The previous approach, where refunds were processed largely on the basis of statutory entitlement and prescribed timelines, has been replaced by a framework driven by compliance behaviour, risk profiling, and data integrity.\n\nUnder the new system, VAT refunds are no longer processed uniformly for claimants. Instead, the approval and timing of refunds are closely linked to a taxpayer\u2019s compliance history, the accuracy and consistency of submitted data, and the level of responsiveness to the Inland Revenue Department (IRD).\n\nFor exporters and other eligible VAT registrants, securing refunds in a timely manner now requires proactive compliance management rather than passive filing. Taxpayers must ensure that returns, schedules, and supporting documentation are accurate, complete, and internally consistent. Discrepancies, omissions, or delays in responding to queries may result in refunds being deferred, suspended, or subjected to further review.\n\nThe shift from the former Simplified Value Added Tax (SVAT) mechanism to the current risk-based refund model reflects a broader move toward data-driven tax administration. As a result, businesses must strengthen internal controls, perform regular reconciliations, and ensure alignment between financial records and tax filings.\n\nIn this evolving environment, VAT refunds are no longer a routine administrative outcome but a compliance-sensitive process. Accordingly, taxpayers must adopt a structured and disciplined approach to compliance management to secure refunds efficiently and minimise the risk of delays, suspensions, or rejections under new VAT refund framework."
  },
  {
