    "md5": "f1b01a257be4b2bce294057df83212f4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "In this evolving environment, VAT refunds are no longer a routine administrative outcome but a compliance-sensitive process. Accordingly, taxpayers must adopt a structured and disciplined approach to compliance management to secure refunds efficiently and minimise the risk of delays, suspensions, or rejections under new VAT refund framework.\n\nUnder the new risk-based VAT refund framework, taxpayers are eligible to claim refunds if they fall into any of the following categories:\n\nExporters: Entities where direct exports account for more than 50% of total supplies in the preceding calendar year.\n\nProjects: Projects approved under Section 22(7) of the VAT Act.\n\nSuppliers to Projects: Suppliers to Special Projects (SP) and Strategic Development Projects (SDP), provided such supplies constitute more than 50% of their total supplies.\n\nThe new framework is designed to ensure timely refunds for genuine exporters and eligible entities while strengthening controls against erroneous or inflated claims.\n\nUnder the new VAT refund framework, eligible registrants are assigned a risk rating that determines the level of scrutiny that a registrant\u2019s VAT return and schedules would be subject to when their refunds are being processed. This categorisation is based on compliance behaviour, including timely registration, accurate reporting, and payments by or before statutory due dates, using a statistically sound risk-assessment methodology.\n\nUnder this scheme, refunds will be issued within 45 days from either the last day of the month following the end of the relevant taxable period, which is the statutory due date or the date of submission of a proper VAT return along with the required schedules for that period, whichever is later, depending on the VAT registrant\u2019s risk categorisation."
  },
  {
