    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2f57fb8fa37f9e1b247dd701997ee84f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1907,
    "chunk_text": "Under this scheme, refunds will be issued within 45 days from either the last day of the month following the end of the relevant taxable period, which is the statutory due date or the date of submission of a proper VAT return along with the required schedules for that period, whichever is later, depending on the VAT registrant\u2019s risk categorisation.\n\nLow risk: Refunds are generally processed promptly without prior verification; audits are conducted post-refund.\n\nMedium risk: Refunds are processed relatively quickly, with standard post-refund verification procedures.\n\nHigh risk: Refunds are subject to pre-verification before processing to ensure accuracy and compliance.\n\nThe risk rating is effective from the taxable period commencing 01 October 2025 and will remain in effect until it is reviewed or cancelled. Ratings will be reviewed every six months, or earlier if deemed necessary. This system allows the IRD to prioritise resources efficiently while encouraging taxpayers to maintain strong compliance records for faster refunds.\n\nRefunds have already begun to be issued to eligible VAT registrants for taxable periods commencing on or after 01 October 2025. The IRD confirmed that up to 90% of the refunds for October 2025, which were scheduled for issuance on 15 January 2026, have been disbursed to all eligible VAT registrants, regardless of their risk rating. Taxpayers may rely on the existing provisions of the VAT Act to claim interest where the IRD fails to issue a refund within the 45-day period (in the absence of any fault on the part of the taxpayer). The taxpayer may invoke Section 59 of the VAT Act and claim interest from the relevant statutory trigger date until the date of payment.\n\nTo secure VAT refunds efficiently under new risk-based system, taxpayers must proactively manage compliance and ensure accuracy across all filings and records. Key considerations include:"
