    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c980e70c09414208b795c9bdcc3b1dde",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1915,
    "chunk_text": "Refunds have already begun to be issued to eligible VAT registrants for taxable periods commencing on or after 01 October 2025. The IRD confirmed that up to 90% of the refunds for October 2025, which were scheduled for issuance on 15 January 2026, have been disbursed to all eligible VAT registrants, regardless of their risk rating. Taxpayers may rely on the existing provisions of the VAT Act to claim interest where the IRD fails to issue a refund within the 45-day period (in the absence of any fault on the part of the taxpayer). The taxpayer may invoke Section 59 of the VAT Act and claim interest from the relevant statutory trigger date until the date of payment.\n\nTo secure VAT refunds efficiently under new risk-based system, taxpayers must proactively manage compliance and ensure accuracy across all filings and records. Key considerations include:\n\nAccuracy of returns and schedules: correctness of reported figures and supporting documentation.\n\nResponsiveness to IRD communications: promptness in providing requested clarifications or documents.\n\nConsistency of reported data across systems: alignment between accounting records, tax filings, and other regulatory submissions.\n\nIn practice, even a single area of weak compliance can affect the processing of valid refund claims. Taxpayers should therefore approach VAT refunds as part of a continuous compliance lifecycle, rather than a one-off administrative transaction.\n\nA common cause of refund delays under the new system is the inconsistency between VAT returns and their supporting schedules. To minimize the risk of delays, taxpayers should ensure that:\n\nInput VAT on imports and local purchases is accurately reported.\n\nExport values declared in returns are fully aligned with Customs data.\n\nEven minor errors or mismatches can trigger system-generated notices, pausing the statutory refund timeline until the necessary corrections are made."
  },
  {
