    "chunk_index": 5,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7334602eb8d2c8b415ce5c1626989c4e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1982,
    "chunk_text": "Poor document management often leads to repeated requests from the IRD and extended verification, even when claims are otherwise valid.\n\nMaintain refund-specific documentation folders (physical or digital) for each taxable period to ensure quick access and avoid last-minute retrieval issues.\n\nVerify supplier registration to ensure they are valid VAT registrants.\n\nIt is critical to verify that suppliers are currently registered for VAT before claiming input VAT. The current practice of the IRD is to place the burden of verification on the claimant (the purchaser), not the supplier. If a supplier is not VAT-registered or has been de-registered, the IRD may disallow the input VAT claim even though the supplier may have charged VAT incorrectly.\n\nBecause of this, businesses must implement internal controls to proactively confirm supplier VAT status prior to claiming input VAT.\n\ni) Obtain a copy of the VAT Registration Certificate during vendor onboarding.\n\nii) Check the IRD\u2019s published list of inactive VAT registrants. Although the list is not updated on a monthly basis, it is revised periodically throughout the year. As the updates are not made in real time, businesses should not rely solely on a one-time review.\n\niii) Ongoing Monitoring. A supplier\u2019s VAT status may change over time, for example due to de-registration. Accordingly, businesses should implement periodic verification procedures as a matter of best practice.\n\nTo support valid input VAT claims, tax invoices must strictly comply with the format and content requirements prescribed by the IRD. Non-compliant invoices may result in disallowed input VAT, even where VAT was charged and paid.\n\nBecause the IRD places the responsibility on the claimant to substantiate input VAT claims, businesses must actively ensure invoice compliance before processing payments or claiming VAT.\n\ni) Request that their TIN (Taxpayer Identification Number) be correctly recorded in the supplier\u2019s customer profile."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-03-31-dailyftlk-9604cb19",
    "num": "dailyftlk-9604cb19",
    "date_str": "2026-03-31",
    "description": "Navigating Sri Lanka\u2019s new VAT refund framework: Key compliance insights",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/columns/Navigating-Sri-Lanka-s-new-VAT-refund-framework-Key-compliance-insights/4-790271",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1774909677.0,
