    "language": "en",
    "md5": "60744a819a2156302d781cbd3604e3c0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1950,
    "chunk_text": "Because the IRD places the responsibility on the claimant to substantiate input VAT claims, businesses must actively ensure invoice compliance before processing payments or claiming VAT.\n\ni) Request that their TIN (Taxpayer Identification Number) be correctly recorded in the supplier\u2019s customer profile.\n\niii) Periodically review supplier records to ensure that all information is accurate and up to date.\n\niv) A critical risk arises when suppliers use dummy, or incorrect numbers either on invoices or on the schedules submitted to the IRD along with the VAT returns. Even if the invoices appear correct, these fictitious numbers can create discrepancies between what the supplier reports and what the claimant has documented, putting the purchaser\u2019s input VAT claim at risk. Since the IRD places the burden of proof on the claimant, it is essential to verify that all invoice numbers, TINs, and reference numbers are legitimate and match the supplier\u2019s official records. Businesses should maintain full supporting documentation, communicate proactively with suppliers to prevent such issues, and flag repeated occurrences for enhanced monitoring to safeguard VAT claims.\n\nv) For suppliers issuing a large volume of invoices, conduct periodic sample reviews to ensure ongoing compliance. Proactive communication is particularly important when automated billing systems generate invoices in bulk, as systemic errors can impact hundreds of transactions.\n\nvii) As far as possible, record input VAT claims promptly rather than waiting until the end of the taxable period.\n\nOutdated taxpayer profiles are a common and avoidable cause of refund delays under the new system. Taxpayers should ensure that the following details are accurately maintained in Revenue Administration Management Information System (RAMIS):\n\nMissed notices or communications due to outdated information can result in suspended refunds, even when claims are otherwise valid."
  },
  {
