    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6b4b28f62c7089e20b19e0ff736bdd43",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1659,
    "chunk_text": "Consulate General\u00a0partners\u00a0Tata Group for Rebuilding Sri Lanka Initiative\n\nFollowing discussions with the Consulate General of Sri Lanka in Mumbai and an on-the-ground assessment in Sri Lanka, the Tata Group has initiated a project to ensure the right to education for children affected by Cyclone Ditwah.\n\nUnder the ONE Tata Sri Lanka Cyclone Ditwah and Floods Response Program, the group has committed to 27,000 learning kits to assist children resume and continue their education. The initiative prioritises humanitarian needs including education and health, particularly in the Districts of Nuwara Eliya, Badulla and Kandy, which were among the worst affected.\n\nThe symbolic handover of the learning kits took place at the Consulate General on 11 March 2026 with the participation of Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles Ltd. Managing Director/CEO Shailesh Chandra, Tata Sons Group Chief Sustainability Officer Chacko Thomas and representatives of the Tata Group companies.\n\nSpeaking at the event, Consul General Priyanga Wickramasinghe said: \u201cThis program stands as a powerful symbol of the enduring partnership between Sri Lanka and India that extends beyond mere diplomacy and statecraft into moments of solidarity during difficult times, and importantly it is a crucial example of the impact which responsible corporate leadership can bring into rebuilding lives. This initiative becomes more meaningful when situated in the context of Sri Lanka\u2019s well-known resilience and the high premium placed on education. Given this national focus on education, the supply of essential learner kits to school children is particularly poignant and thoughtful.\u201d"
  },
